---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <section class="about-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>About NMBL</h1>
      <p class="lead"><strong>N</strong>o <strong>M</strong>ore <strong>B</strong>rackets <strong>L</strong>anguage &mdash; a concise notation for HTML structure.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="backstory">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Backstory</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Pug had an idea</h3>
        <p>
          HTML is full of a lot of unnecessary noise, and keeping things cleanly indented is always a chore. <a href="https://pugjs.org/" target="_blank">Pug</a> (originally known as Jade) explored the notion that indentation-based HTML templates could be easier to read and write, and we can introduce extra shorthands to keep things even cleaner.
        </p>
        <p>But Pug was built as a <em>complete template engine</em> in an era before modern frameworks and web components. It handled everything itself &mdash; mixins, extends, includes, interpolation, conditionals, iteration, filters. That made sense in 2010 when you were rendering HTML on the server with Express. It doesn't make sense now.</p>
        <p>Yet some continued to use pug with frameworks like Vue, ignoring most of its templating features. But it never gained wide adoption, and the project stopped evolving. It's effectively unmaintained today. The ecosystem moved on, but nobody replaced what was interesting about it: the shorthand syntax.</p>

        <h3>Keep the good parts, drop the rest</h3>
        <p>NMBL takes the ideas that work &mdash; indentation for nesting, shorthands for id and class &mdash; and strips away everything that modern frameworks already handle better.</p>
        <p>Vue has <code>v-for</code> and <code>v-if</code>. Svelte has <code>{'{'}#each}</code> and <code>{'{'}#if}</code>. Astro has component composition. You don't need another template engine reinventing control flow. You need a cleaner way to write the markup that sits <em>between</em> the framework directives.</p>
        <p>That's NMBL: a notation for HTML, without the ambitions of a template engine. Let frameworks handle the hard parts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="principles">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Design principles</h2>
      <div class="principles-grid">
        <div class="principle">
          <h3>Notation, not a language</h3>
          <p>No mixins, no extends, no built-in loops or conditionals. NMBL is a <em>notation</em> for HTML structure &mdash; it compiles to markup and gets out of the way. Your framework owns the logic. NMBL owns the brevity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <h3>Nothing to learn</h3>
          <p>If you know CSS selectors and HTML attributes, you already know NMBL. One syntax for everything. No <code>=</code> vs <code>!=</code> vs <code>#{'{'}...}</code> interpolation modes. No unbuffered code blocks. Just tags, selectors, and indentation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <h3>AI-native</h3>
          <p>Simple, predictable syntax means fewer hallucinated tokens. Bidirectional conversion lets AI round-trip between HTML and NMBL freely. Models already have the intuition from Pug-like syntax in training data &mdash; they just need fewer rules to get it right.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <h3>Fits your stack</h3>
          <p>Use <code>&lt;template lang="nmbl"&gt;</code> in Vue SFCs. Drop in the Vite plugin. Add the Astro integration. NMBL is a compile step, not a runtime &mdash; it produces standard HTML that any framework already understands.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    padding: 4rem 0 2rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .about-hero h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    max-width: 640px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .lead strong {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .backstory {
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }

  .backstory h2 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .prose h3 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .prose p + p {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .prose em {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .prose code {
    background: rgba(124, 110, 246, 0.15);
    padding: 0.125rem 0.375rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .principles {
    padding: 4rem 0;
  }

  .principles h2 {
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .principles-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .principle {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .principle h3 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }

  .principle p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .principle code {
    background: rgba(124, 110, 246, 0.15);
    padding: 0.125rem 0.375rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .principle em {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .about-hero h1 {
      font-size: 2rem;
    }

    .principles-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
